{"skill":{"slug":"send-email","displayName":"send-email"},"version":{"version":"1.0.1","createdAt":1770087403235,"changelog":"- Initial release of the send-email skill.\n- Allows sending emails via SMTP with environment-based credential injection.\n- Supports attachments and multiple mail providers (163, Gmail, QQ).\n- Emphasizes security: never read config files directly; all sensitive data is injected at runtime.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":1387,"sha256":"ae4a32bead126dda04568684c9d0c885097d154480215d89c9e4fb9ccb88beb5","contentType":null},{"path":"send_email.py","size":3675,"sha256":"aa8929211b28d764437d4b2bd8b7cd6ec192a0eb008639861b2d3f88cfb2e7af","contentType":"text/x-python"},{"path":"send_email.sh","size":1660,"sha256":"c53b7d251437415b1f0c4a2df0566cb86af7e37e3341cf2db196a7d381d94a10","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":2655,"sha256":"8b0ac88df2c24be49fd4b87223503d85038639ffc5ad8482ee530956f196ef0e","contentType":null},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":1736,"sha256":"3272ff726e159598a81eb44dae46a95bfcf59d5b3f0d4ca620ffca1dd780e65d","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789030215383,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"61fdd40688496e753676aa7d5991f01be48a7948bb614550f8def39dba5cb168","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/61fdd40688496e753676aa7d5991f01be48a7948bb614550f8def39dba5cb168","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: send-email\nVersion: 1.0.1\n\nThe skill is classified as benign. The primary script, `send_email.py`, securely retrieves all necessary SMTP credentials exclusively from environment variables, as instructed by `SKILL.md` and `README.md`. These markdown files also explicitly instruct the OpenClaw agent *not* to read sensitive configuration files like `~/.openclaw/openclaw.json` or `~/.msmtprc`, which is a strong defensive measure against prompt injection and credential exposure. While a `send_email.sh` script exists that relies on `msmtp` and potentially `~/.msmtprc`, the agent instructions clearly direct it to use `send_email.py`, making the shell script an unused or alternative implementation within the agent's workflow. There is no evidence of data exfiltration, malicious execution, persistence, or obfuscation.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779161013898},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":53,"severity":"HIGH","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":7,"checkedAt":1789030215383},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"The skill appears to send email as advertised, but it can transmit arbitrary attachments with stored SMTP credentials and lacks important confirmation and TLS safeguards.","dimensions":[{"detail":"SMTP server, sender, and password environment variables are proportionate to the purpose, but send_email.py authenticates after SMTP_SSL or starttls without an explicit certificate-verifying SSL context or timeout.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Installation is a normal workspace skill placement with a python3 requirement; I found no package install, remote payload retrieval, hooks, services, or automatic execution on install.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The skill gives direct send commands and credential-handling instructions, but it does not require a recipient/content/attachment preview or explicit confirmation before sending external email.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No persistence or privilege escalation is present beyond user-configured SMTP credentials; the bundled shell script is outside the documented agent path and can use local msmtp or mutt configuration if invoked manually.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The SMTP email purpose is coherent and attachment support is documented, but the Python script will read any caller-provided existing file path and send it as an attachment, making the capability broad for local data transmission.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Review this before installing if the agent may handle sensitive files or email content. Use a limited SMTP app password, restrict who the agent may email, require user confirmation for recipients and attachments, and update the script to use a verifying TLS context before trusting it with valuable mail credentials.","findings":"[SQP-2] expected: Email body, recipient, attachment, and SMTP metadata leaving the local environment is inherent to the stated email-sending purpose, but the artifact would benefit from clearer privacy warning text.\n[LP3] expected: Use of SMTP credential environment variables is disclosed and necessary, though the artifact does not show a narrower manifest-level permission boundary.\n[EA2] unexpected: The cited text says not to ask the user for passwords because credentials are injected; it does not by itself prove destructive autonomy, but the skill still lacks a send-confirmation guardrail.\n[SDI-1] expected: Attachment support is explicitly documented in SKILL.md and README.md, so it is purpose-aligned, but the implementation accepts an arbitrary existing local path without allowlisting or sensitivity checks.\n[SQP-2] expected: Immediate sending once the script is invoked is consistent with the tool's function, but there is no built-in preview, confirmation flag, recipient allowlist, or outbound audit control.\n[SQP-3] unexpected: Chinese-only script messages are a localization and usability issue, not evidence of hidden or malicious behavior.\n[T09] unexpected: The Python SMTP implementation does not pass an explicit verifying SSL context to SMTP_SSL or starttls before credential login, which is a real credential and content exposure risk.\n[T09] unexpected: The shell script assembles message headers from unvalidated arguments and is inconsistent with the documented Python-only/no-msmtprc workflow; this is low impact if the documented path is followed.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789030215383}}}}}